---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ReversePdfPageOrder from '@/components/tools/pdf/ReversePdfPageOrder.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="reverse-pdf-page-order">
  <ReversePdfPageOrder client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Three ways a PDF ends up backwards</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A face-down scan: feed a stack face-down into a sheet feeder and the scanner works from the bottom
          of the pile up, so the document comes out in reverse.
        </li>
        <li>
          A face-up printer stack: some printers deliver pages face-up, so the last page is on top. Scan that
          pile and you get a reversed PDF.
        </li>
        <li>
          A right-to-left original: an Arabic, Hebrew or Japanese document assembled with Western page order
          reads back to front for its readers.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can reverse the PDF page order in one click in all three cases, without a new scan.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The three reverse modes</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Mode</th>
            <th scope="col">1,2,3,4,5,6 becomes</th>
            <th scope="col">Use it when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Every page</td>
            <td>6,5,4,3,2,1</td>
            <td>The whole document is back to front</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sheets, fronts and backs together</td>
            <td>5,6,3,4,1,2</td>
            <td>A duplex stack came out in the wrong sheet order</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Only a range, e.g. 3-6</td>
            <td>1,2,6,5,4,3</td>
            <td>An appendix or exhibit set was scanned backwards</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        In a double-sided document, pages come in pairs: 1 and 2 are the two faces of one sheet. If you
        reverse every page, the pairs separate and the backs come before the fronts. Sheet mode reverses
        the order of the sheets and keeps each pair together, which is what a mis-stacked duplex run needs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The order preview</h2>
      <p>
        Under the options, a strip of numbered cards shows the new sequence: new position on top, original
        page number below. Check it in range mode, where a range is easily off by one. The tool writes
        nothing until you press the button.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What reversing does not change</h2>
      <p>
        The tool copies each page into the output document as a complete object: its content stream, fonts,
        images, annotations and text layer. Only the page tree, the list that sets the page order, changes.
        Resolution, compression, colour and searchable text stay identical, and the file size barely changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bookmarks and internal cross-reference links point at specific page objects. They stay attached to
        their pages, so a bookmark to &ldquo;Chapter 3&rdquo; still goes to chapter 3, which is now near the
        front of the document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reversing versus reordering</h2>
      <p>
        For any other new order, such as moving page 12 to the front, dropping a section or interleaving two
        files, use <a href="/organize-pdf">Organize PDF</a>. It has drag-and-drop page thumbnails.
        <a href="/merge-alternate-pdf-pages">Merge Alternate Pages</a> handles the two-file duplex case.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
